# __SITE_SLUG__

__SITE_NAME__ — an app on the affiliate engine, scaffolded by `create-site`.

This is a **thin app shell**: all niche-agnostic logic lives in the engine
packages (`@affiliate-engine/core`, `@affiliate-engine/ui`,
`@affiliate-engine/sanity-schema`). The app provides:

- **Sanity wiring** (`src/lib/sanity/*`) — the public/write clients, GROQ
  queries, and the engine port implementations (`ProductReviewProvider`,
  `CatalogStore`, `BlogStore`) backed by the real Sanity client.
- **Config injection** — `src/app/providers.tsx` wraps the tree in the engine's
  `FrameworkProvider` (real `next/link` / `next/image` / `next/navigation`) and
  `SiteConfigProvider`.
- **Thin route shells** — `src/app/**` pages/route handlers that call the engine
  + UI.

## ⚠️ Fill in the SiteConfig before going live

`src/site.config.ts` is a **valid but skeletal** `SiteConfig` full of `TODO`
placeholders. Replace them with real values:

- brand (name, domain, affiliate tag, founders, tagline),
- theme palette (and the matching tokens in `src/app/globals.css`),
- scoring axes (factor weights must sum to 1.0 per axis),
- segmentation buckets,
- taxonomy categories,
- editorial copy and gift guides.

Do **not** fabricate real-world data (products, ASINs, affiliate links, images):
those are added later through the verified-content pipeline.

> The plural product noun is baked into the `src/app/(public)/best-__PRODUCT_NOUN_PLURAL__`
> route directory and must stay in sync with `SiteConfig.product.nounPlural`
> (the engine's navigation + sitemap build `/best-<noun>` paths). If you change
> the plural noun, rename that directory to match.

## Environment

See `.env.example`. A full `next build` works without live credentials (data
routes degrade gracefully); production needs `NEXT_PUBLIC_SANITY_PROJECT_ID`,
`SANITY_API_TOKEN`, `DATABASE_URL`, and `CRON_SECRET`.

## Scripts

- `dev`, `build`, `start`, `lint`, `type-check`, `test`

## Security notes

- Cron routes (`/api/cron/*`) require `Authorization: Bearer ${CRON_SECRET}`.
- The Sanity webhook verifies an HMAC signature (`SANITY_WEBHOOK_SECRET`).
- The admin dashboards (`/dashboard/*`) are **unauthenticated** out of the box.
  Place them behind auth (e.g. middleware) or network-restrict them before
  exposing the site publicly.
